# There is no modem here and no site to call, so what these show
# is their own face: the prompt, the settings, the log.

shot    aterm
cap     ATerm 2.6, the terminal emulator, reading its configuration from
cap     SYS/ATERM.
run     /dd/CMDS/aterm
wait    6

shot    aterm-cfg
for     aterm setterm fkeys initvdu cls
cap     The terminal side: ATerm's configuration, setterm, fkeys and the
cap     two small things that clear a screen.
run     /dd/CMDS/setterm -?
wait    5
run     /dd/CMDS/fkeys -?
wait    5
run     /dd/CMDS/cls
wait    4
run     /dd/CMDS/tty
wait    5

shot    elm
for     elm frm newmail readmsg printmail messages
cap     Elm, the mail reader, opening on an empty mailbox -- which is the
cap     honest state of a mail system with no network.  Fourteen programs
cap     travel with it in CMDS/ELM.
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/elm
wait    10
quit    q\r

shot    elm-suite
for     elm answer arepdaemon autoreply fastmail filter newalias printmail readmsg messages
cap     The rest of the elm suite: the alias tools, the filter, the
cap     auto-responder and the daemon that answers while you are away.
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/newalias
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/printmail
wait    8

shot    finger
for     finger net osknet atp msntp infoxpress
cap     The network set.  There is no interface configured here, so what
cap     they show is what they would do if there were.
run     /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/finger
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/net
wait    8

shot    frm
for     frm checkalias listalias newalias filter autoreply answer fastmail
cap     The rest of the elm suite says what it wants: frm lists the from
cap     lines of a folder, the alias tools read the alias files.
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/frm
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/listalias
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/ELM/checkalias root
wait    6

shot    kermit
cap     Kermit, the file-transfer program.  With no serial line to talk to
cap     it says what it can do: connect, send, receive, or be the server.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/kermit
wait    5

shot    kermit-family
for     kermit2 kermit3 xkermit ckermit
cap     Four more Kermits.  The collection keeps every build it found, and
cap     they do not all take the same command line -- which is the reason
cap     to keep them side by side.
run     /dd/CMDS/kermit2
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/xkermit
wait    8

shot    mail
for     mail lmail rmail smail
cap     The mail chain: mail sends, lmail delivers locally, rmail takes
cap     what arrived, smail routes.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/mail -?
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/smail -?
wait    6

shot    mailx
cap     mailx opening with no mail waiting -- which is the honest state of
cap     a mail reader on a disk with no network.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/mailx
wait    5
send    q\r
wait    3

shot    mailx-more
for     mailx philmail dotilde makedb pathalias
cap     mailx opening on an empty mailbox, and philmail beside it.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/mailx
wait    8
send    q\r
wait    6

shot    network
for     finger msntp atp osknet infoxpress net
cap     The TCP/IP set: finger, an SNTP client, and OSKnet itself.  There
cap     is no interface here, so what they print is what they would do.
run     /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/msntp -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/NETWORK/atp
wait    8

shot    news-tools
for     rnews postnews subscribe unsubscribe expire uulog
cap     What arrives from a news feed and what unpacks it: rnews takes a
cap     batch, expire deletes what is past its date.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/rnews -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/subscribe -?
wait    8

shot    newshist
for     newshist bdecode byteflip c7decode dbz newslock
cap     CMDS/NEWS: the pieces that unpack a usenet news batch -- decoders,
cap     the history database and the lock.
run     /dd/CMDS/NEWS/dbz
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/NEWS/bdecode
wait    8

shot    pathalias
for     pathalias makedb nptx dotilde uuclean
cap     pathalias computes mail routes from a map of who calls whom -- the
cap     program that made a usenet address possible.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/pathalias -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/makedb -?
wait    6

shot    readnews
cap     readnews, the news reader, with no spool to read.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/readnews
wait    5
send    q\r
wait    3

shot    screen
for     screen tsmon2 connect uwho wysetime
cap     screen, the terminal multiplexer, and tsmon2, which is what watches
cap     a serial line for someone logging in.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/screen -v
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/tsmon2 -?
wait    8

shot    setterm
cap     setterm and cls, the two smallest things here that know what a
cap     terminal is.
run     /dd/CMDS/setterm
wait    4

shot    sterm
for     sterm tterm tsu k z blastem dld uld vmod_trap
cap     The terminal programs: sterm and tterm open a serial line and give
cap     you the far end.  With no line configured they say so.
run     /dd/CMDS/COMMS/tterm
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/COMMS/tsu
wait    8

shot    uuclean
for     uuclean uupoll disable enable fileserv fixtext lcasep nptx pwparse
cap     The UUCP housekeeping: clean the spool, poll a site, take a device
cap     out of service and put it back.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uuclean -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uupoll -?
wait    8

shot    uucp
for     uucp uux uuxqt uucico
cap     The transfer set: uucp queues a copy, uux queues a command, uuxqt
cap     runs what a remote site queued here, uucico does the talking.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uucp -?
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uucico -?
wait    6

shot    uuname
cap     uuname lists the UUCP sites this system could reach, out of the
cap     configuration in SYS.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uuname
wait    4
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uulog
wait    4

shot    uwho
for     uwho udate input
cap     What is left of the UNAXCESS bulletin board: uwho wants /etc/utmp,
cap     which OS-9 has no equivalent of.
run     /dd/CMDS/uwho
wait    6
run     /dd/CMDS/udate
wait    6

shot    wn
for     wn wndex authwn inetd inetdc
cap     WN, a web server, and its indexer -- on a 68000 OS-9 disk.  It is
cap     configured to be started by inetd, which is also here.
run     /dd/CMDS/WN/wn -V
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/WN/wndex -?
wait    8

shot    xmodem
for     xy xydown xyt txmod rxmod
cap     The xmodem and ymodem set from CMDS/COMMS -- send, receive, and the
cap     terminal side of a file transfer over a serial line.
run     /dd/CMDS/COMMS/xydown
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/COMMS/txmod
wait    8


shot    terminals
for     cls fkeys initvdu setterm wysecrack wysetime
cap     The small terminal utilities, asked what they take.  `fkeys' and
cap     `initvdu' print NOTHING at all -- no usage, no error -- which is
cap     what the two blank lines under their names are.  The Wyse pair want
cap     real Wyse hardware.
run     /dd/CMDS/setterm -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/fkeys -?
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/wysecrack
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/wysetime -?
wait    8
